_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 8
---
order: 8
---
short_label: VI.8
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
----
statement: If in a right-angled triangle a perpendicular is drawn from the right angle to the base, then the triangles adjoining the perpendicular are similar both to the whole and to one another.
----
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCt} be a right-angled triangle having the angle {BAC|rtBAC} right, and let {AD} be drawn from {A} perpendicular to {BC}.

I say that each of the triangles {DBA} and {DAC} is similar to the whole {ABC|ABCt}, and, further, they are similar to one another.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="330" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.8 diagram" src="propVI8.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The right-angled triangle ABC, the perpendicular AD, and the two
    // triangles DBA and DAC it cuts off; both similarities go through I.32
    // and VI.4 to VI.Def.1.
    //
    // Joyce's figure keeps A on the semicircle on BC (the circle, named
    // ABC, is a transparent helper) and fills the two small triangles ABD
    // and ACD; the whole triangle has no element of its own. Everything
    // added leaves the resting figure as it was. ABCt is a solid outline of
    // the whole triangle with no face, hidden at rest (initiallyHidden), so
    // the opening slides can show ABC before the perpendicular is drawn; AD
    // is a solid twin of the perpendicular, hidden at rest, so the slide
    // strokes it from A. The sides AB, AC, BC, BD, CD are transparent
    // highlight targets, and DBA and DAC are faceless twins of the filled
    // triangles: a highlighted filled polygon is raised (#140) and would
    // cover the angle markers, so captions and prose name the twins.
    //
    // Markers: the right angles BAC, BDA, ADC (by hypothesis and by the
    // perpendicular) are red squares; equal angles share a colour — ACB and
    // DAB orange, the angle at B and DAC blue. At A the two parts DAB and
    // DAC lie side by side inside the right angle, so all three are pinned
    // to opt out of ring stepping; the two squares at D are pinned too.
    // Angle and triangle names collide (DAC): the triangle owns it, the
    // angle takes the display override.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): B, C free and A a
    // circleSlider — the given triangle's vertices, all on slide 1, so
    // nothing is deferred. D is derived (`foot`) and listed per slide.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","ABCt","rtBAC"];
    var PERP  = GIVEN.concat(["AD","D","rtBDA","rtADC"]);
    var TRI   = PERP.concat(["ABD","ACD","AB","AC","BC","BD","CD","DBA","DAC"]);
    var COMB  = TRI.concat(["angB"]);
    var REMC  = COMB.concat(["angC","angDAB"]);
    var REMB  = REMC.concat(["angDAC"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "VI.8",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;40,170",
            "C;point;free;290,170",
            "M;point;midpoint;B,C;0;0",
            "ABC;circle;radius;M,B;0;0;0;0",
            "A;point;circleSlider;ABC,200,40",
            "D;point;foot;A,B,C",
            "ABD;polygon;triangle;A,B,D;0;0;black;random",
            "ACD;polygon;triangle;A,C,D;0;0;black;random",
            // The whole triangle as a solid, faceless outline, and the
